# Review ownership for this repository.
#
# This file routes nothing today, and saying so is the point of writing it down. The repository
# has one collaborator, who is also the author of every pull request opened against it, and GitHub
# does not request review from a pull request's own author. Requiring code owner approval is a
# branch protection setting, and branch protection became available when this repository was
# published on 2026-08-16. It now requires the three CI checks and refuses force pushes and branch
# deletion, and it does not require review, because with one collaborator that would block a merge.
#
# What it does do is state which areas need the most careful reading, so that a second reviewer,
# whenever there is one, inherits the judgement instead of rediscovering it. The areas below are
# the ones where a mistake is expensive rather than the ones that change most often, and the
# ordering matters: a later match wins, so the narrowest rules are last.

# Everything, unless a later line takes precedence.
*                               @raymond-nassar

# Automation. A workflow change cannot be verified by reading the diff, because the thing it
# changes is what runs on the next push rather than what the file says.
/.github/workflows/             @raymond-nassar
/.github/dependabot.yml         @raymond-nassar
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @raymond-nassar

# Persistence. One browser storage key holds everything a reader has, and no server can put it
# back. A defect here is the most expensive kind this project can ship.
/src/js/storage.js              @raymond-nassar
/src/js/cache.js                @raymond-nassar
/test/storage.test.js           @raymond-nassar
/test/cache-db.test.js          @raymond-nassar
/test/concurrent-tabs.test.js   @raymond-nassar

# Recovery. Twice now the most dangerous code in a change was the code added to prevent data
# loss: it runs only when something has already gone wrong, so it is the least exercised path in
# the app and the one review has to work hardest on.
/test/backup-bounds.test.js     @raymond-nassar
/test/recovery-copy.test.js     @raymond-nassar
/src/dev-faults.js              @raymond-nassar

# Generated data. These files are written by the vendoring scripts and read by the app, so a
# hand edit here is invisible until the next regeneration silently reverts it.
/src/data/                      @raymond-nassar
/scripts/vendor-orders.mjs      @raymond-nassar
/scripts/vendor-index.mjs       @raymond-nassar
/scripts/build-event-order.mjs  @raymond-nassar

# Release metadata. The version number describes stored data rather than features, so getting it
# wrong tells a reader their backup is safe to skip when it is not.
/package.json                   @raymond-nassar
/src/js/lib/version.js          @raymond-nassar
/scripts/sync-version.mjs       @raymond-nassar
/CHANGELOG.md                   @raymond-nassar
